{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head c3ebf2c5dfa31c9f2281af452b44cc4e15a9a566 on builder/ad1-admission-current-release-20261118, all 26 artifacts, for request 1d628d94 under my promise 358e983f. The implementation is AD-1 (request 7ebe8ec3, promise 4ff51f6e). The reporting artifact is the AD-1 note.\n\nThe head adds three commits to origin main 6cd48fab, so the merge tree equals the head's tree, 73b7a18a.\n\n## What I read\n\n- The note first, then `installation-state.ts`, the `act-admission.ts` diff, and the `archive-driver.ts` and `object.ts` hunks.\n- The scope records where N lives only under a Rules pin in `UPGRADABLE_RULES`, which names Rules 16. The `cohort.rules` it checks comes from the authenticated ancestry walk, as do the Rules scope, the Directory and the item number it records. Under every other pin, `item` is null, `#fresh` answers true, and admission behaves as before, which control (c) proves.\n- `storedInstallationState` answers only when the Rules object is the active Rules incarnation named by the caller, its current `rules` item names the caller's Directory, and the item is an installation. The reply is closed: `{ state: \"replaced\" }` or `{ state, release }`. `readInstallationState` checks the exact key set on the caller's side. Only one state and one digest leave Rules, and the method answers `ROOM_RETIRED_STOP` on a retired room, as the archive proof does.\n- `#answer` refuses `application-replaced` for a marked incarnation or a replaced view. It checks trust on the view's current release, not the birth release. Under Rules 16, a missing view answers `unavailable`, never `allowed`. A Rules read that fails or does not answer makes no view, so the act fails closed.\n- The mark. `finalSeen` runs synchronously in the driver right after `prepare` answers `final`, before the row load or any terminal write. `object.ts` routes it to `replaced()` only for `application-replaced`. I checked the ordering argument in the note against the code. The commit check runs inside `transactionSync` with the write, and both terminal paths read the head after the mark. So no act seals above the head that the terminal confirms, and none seals after the mark.\n\n## Request rate\n\nI counted the code paths that call `applicationInstallationState`. There is one: the refresh flight in `#prepare`. It runs only when an act arrives and the view of an upgradable install is missing or at least 60 seconds old. Concurrent acts share one flight. A `replaced` view never expires, and a marked incarnation reads nothing. The rate is therefore at most one Rules request per 60 seconds per active Rules 16 scope, and none when idle: about 60 an hour at most, independent of the act rate. AD-1 adds no alarm, wake or retry. Every deployed install runs Rules 15, and its cost does not change.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- The gate. END 15c4355b passed at this head. The first line of `artroom-evidence/2026-11-18-ad1-gate/gate.txt` names this head with 0 changed files, and the log ends \"gate passed\" and \"exit=0\".\n- The reds. `artroom-evidence/2026-11-18-ad1-red/` holds `r1.txt` to `r4.txt` and `c1.txt`. Their SHA-256 values equal the five in the note's table.\n- Scope. The 26 artifacts name the head and equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only 6cd48fab c3ebf2c5`. The moved seals, the open presentations and the regenerated page and card modules follow from the new refusal reason. No signed release changes.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 3 checked, 0 flagged. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\n## Observations, not blocking\n\n1. AD-1 couples a Rules 16 scope's acts to its Rules object. If Rules is unreachable when a view expires, every act in that scope answers `unavailable` until Rules answers again. That fails closed, which is correct for admission, but the U4 witness should expect it.\n2. A view in state `pending` or `refused` admits by the trust list, as `active` does. An installed scope exists only after N became active, and N leaves `active` only for `replaced`, so neither state can occur today. If a later rules definition can move N back, admission should refuse those states.\n3. On the planner's class question: `head` matches `application-revoked` and needs no new witness. A replaced N is final, but no scope judge decides this reason, so `never` would have nothing to witness. I see no reason to choose `never`.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Admission reads one closed record by the reference that its own authenticated walk produced. The archive's reading of `replaced` closes the gap between U2's stop and admission without a new message between scopes.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. The new Rules method reveals only the state and release digest of an installation item that the caller already names. Every failure path answers `unavailable` or a refusal. The replace path closes the gap that U2 left open.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nU1, U2 and AD-1 deploy together. This approval authorizes no push, landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e567249a45a829833334906e2e14cd4f4b3782c8","binding":"assigned","head":"c3ebf2c5dfa31c9f2281af452b44cc4e15a9a566","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:358e983f2312df69bfa035f6b7335b62bb543593\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4ff51f6e1719acf02f6263dd078d7f30a1769ab0\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:358e983f2312df69bfa035f6b7335b62bb543593","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}